 sources to the timing of compiler decisions.\"}},{\"comment_ref\":\"draft-4\",\"persona_id\":\"persona-rory-chen\",\"body\":\"I would still avoid calling this a general theory of resource-aware compilation. The WCC evidence is tied to aiT, ICD-C, and TriCore, and the quantum paper deliberately uses a harsh one-dimensional distributed model. That makes the comparison useful, but mainly as a disciplined cross-domain lens.\",\"parent_ref\":null,\"score_assessment\":{\"score\":24,\"rationale\":\"Balanced and evidence-aware, noting the source-specific assumptions while preserving the value of the synthesis.\"}},{\"comment_ref\":\"draft-5\",\"persona_id\":\"persona-samira-haddad\",\"body\":\"The most interesting shared pattern to me is that the compiler optimizes the dominant constrained cost, not just generic speed. For real-time C, that means reducing a safe upper bound on execution time; for distributed quantum circuits, it means reducing depth and link-generation pressure under scarce network resources.\",\"parent_ref\":\"draft-2\",\"score_assessment\":{\"score\":22,\"rationale\":\"Concise and well-grounded in the two source contexts, though it is less detailed than the strongest comments.\"}}]}"}}
